In the last video, you learned about the softmax layer in the softmax activation function.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.500
In this video, you'll deepen your understanding of softmax classification and also learn

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.260
how to train a model that uses a softmax layer.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.680
Recall our earlier example where the output layer computes ZL as follows.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.740
So if you have four classes, C equals 4, then ZLs can be 4 by 1 dimensional vector.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.340
And we said we compute T, which is this temporary variable that performs element-wise

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.300
exponentiation.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.500
And then finally, if the activation function for your output layer, G of L, is the softmax

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.780
activation function, then the output will be this.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.720
It's basically taking the temporary variable T and normalizing it to sum to 1.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:52.140
So this then becomes A of L. So you notice that in the Z vector, the biggest element

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.740
was 5, and the biggest probability ends up being this first probability.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:04.220
The name softmax comes from contrasting it to what's called a hardmax, which would have

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.460
taken the vector Z and mapped it to this vector.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:14.140
So hardmax function will look at the elements of Z and just put a 1 in the position of

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.500
the biggest element of Z and then 0s everywhere else.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:23.700
And so this is a very hardmax, where the biggest element gets the output of 1 and

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.260
everything else gets the output of 0.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:33.620
Whereas in contrast, the softmax is a more gentle mapping from Z to these probabilities.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.980
So I'm not sure if this is a great name, but at least that was the intuition behind why

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.500
we called a softmax.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.220
It's in contrast to the hardmax.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:48.100
And one thing I didn't really show, but alluded to, is that softmax regression, or

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:54.220
the softmax activation function, generalizes the logistic activation function to C classes

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.420
rather than just two classes.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:05.780
And it turns out that if C is equal to 2, then softmax with C equals to 2 essentially reduces

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.180
to logistic regression.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.940
And I'm not going to prove this in this video, but the rough outline for the proof is that

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:21.500
if C equals to 2 and if you apply softmax, then the output layer, AL, will output two

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.980
numbers of C equals 2.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.180
So maybe it outputs 0.842 and 0.158, right?

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.660
And these two numbers always have to sum to 1.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.740
And because these two numbers always have to sum to 1, they're actually redundant and

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.700
maybe you don't need to bother to compute two of them.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.300
Maybe you just need to compute one of them.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:45.260
And it turns out that the way you end up computing that number reduces to the way that

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.020
logistic regression is computing this single output.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:55.580
So that wasn't much of a proof, but the takeaway from this is that softmax regression is a generalization

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.660
of logistic regression to more than two classes.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.500
Now let's look at how you would actually train a neural network with a softmax output

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.500
layer.

