So in particular, let's define the loss function you use to train your neural network.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.540
Let's take an example.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.620
Let's say you have an example in your training set where the output is, with the target

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.400
output, the ground truth label is 0 1 0 0.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.280
So the example from the previous video, this means that this is an image of a cat because

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.000
it falls into class 1.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.320
And now let's say that your neural network is currently outputting y hat equals, so y

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.960
hat would be a vector probability, it's going to sum to 1, 0.1, 0.4.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:43.000
So you can check that sums to 1, and this is going to be a L.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.640
So the neural network is not doing very well in this example because this is actually

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.760
a cat and assigned only a 20% chance that this is a cat.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.400
So it didn't do very well in this example.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.840
So what's the loss function you would want to use to train this neural network?

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:02.800
In softmax classification, the loss we typically use is negative sum of j equals 1 through

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:10.120
4, and it's really sum from 1 to c in the general case, and I just use 4 here, of y

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.720
log of yj log y hat of j.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:20.360
So let's look at our single example above to better understand what happens.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:30.080
Notice that in this example, y1 equals y3 equals y4 equals 0 because those are zeros and only

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.120
y2 is equal to 1.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:39.880
So if you look at this summation, all the terms with zero values of yj equal to 0, and

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:48.280
the only term you're left with is negative y2 log y hat 2 because when you sum over the

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:52.960
indices of j, all the terms will end up 0 except when j is equal to 2.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:58.700
And because y2 is equal to 1, this is just negative log y hat 2.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.600
So what this means is that if your learning algorithm is trying to make this small, because

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:09.720
you use gradient descent to try to reduce the loss on your training set, then the only

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.200
way to make this small is to make this small, and the only way to do that is to make y hat

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.680
2 as big as possible.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.480
And these are probabilities, so it can never be bigger than 1, but this kind of makes sense

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:29.460
because if x, for this example, is a picture of a cat, then you want that output probability

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.280
to be as big as possible.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.600
So more generally, what this loss function does is it looks at whatever is the ground

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.520
truth clause in your training set, and it tries to make the corresponding probability

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.760
of that clause as high as possible.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.960
If you're familiar with maximum likelihood estimation in statistics, this turns out to

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.640
